From 104ea9eaaa59556da32e6c01708c60b437a0a0ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ccicnce113424 <ccicnce113424@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 12:02:47 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 10/12] nvidia-x11: build each driver in a factory and expose
 it as a scope

default.nix is a factory: called once by pkgs it builds the driver and the
userspace tools, called again by linuxPackages with a kernel it adds the kernel
modules to the same branch. pkgs.nvidiaPackages.production.driver and
pkgs.linuxPackages.nvidiaPackages.production.driver are the same derivation.

nvidiaPackages.<branch> is an attribute set, with the driver as .driver and the
tools as siblings. A branch is a lib.makeExtensible set rather than a scope,
because lib.makeScope hands out a callPackage bound to the scope, and taking it
forces every member, which leaves no room for the removed output names below.

The four removed output names warn and return .driver.<name> where the driver
has that output or passthru entry. Where it has neither, selecting the name
fails like any missing attribute. .modsrc always throws. legacy_340 and
legacy_390 have no lib32 on a host that builds no i686, and those two plus
legacy_470 have no firmware.

modprobeSha256 loses its default: every branch, including one built with
mkDriver, has to carry nvidia-modprobe, because the module needs it for its udev
rules. kernel-modules.nix and the four tool files take supportedPlatforms rather
than platforms, since callPackage fills a parameter of that name from
pkgs.platforms, which is an attrset, while meta.platforms has to be a list.

hardware.nvidia.package is the branch, an extensible set rather than a scope,
and hardware.nvidia.kernelModules is the module built for the configured kernel,
following package and hardware.nvidia.open. Its defaultText states the rule
rather than the resolved value, since the lookup goes through an internal
binding.

README.md describes the resulting layout.

The nvidia_x11 aliases keep the branch's tools and modules, so a consumer that
read .settings or .mod off one still builds, and the warning names the new path.
The next commit moves the in-tree consumers over.

Assisted-by: omp:Space Bunny Alpha
